          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            %em wave front set
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander70"}> Hörmander 70
            ) of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+function"} generalized function
            such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperfunction"} hyperfunction
            is a characterization of the singularity structure of the generalized function, hence of how it deviates from being an ordinary smooth function.
          %p
            The wave front set is the sub-bundle of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            that consists of all those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction+of+a+vector"} directions
            (non-zero
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covectors"} covectors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
            of the distribution is not rapidly decaying in this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction+of+a+vector"} direction
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, section 8.1
            ). Such covectors are stable under multiplication by positive scalars, so the wave front set can also be considered as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sub-bundle"} sub-bundle
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+sphere+bundle"} unit sphere bundle
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            of the wave front set down to the base space is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support+of+a+distribution"} singular support
            of the distribution. The additional information in the “wave front”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covectors"} covectors
            over this singular support may be understood as providing the directions of
            = succeed "." do
              %em propagation of these singularities
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of singularities theorem
          %p
            A notorious issue with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            is that, when thought of as generalized functions, generally neither their
            %span.newWikiWord
              composition of distributions
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/composition+of+distributions"} ?
            nor their pointwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
            is defined. However, closer inspection shows that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
            to these operations being defined for any given pair of distributions is exactly characterized by the wave front set:
          %p
            For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
            is well defined precisely if the sum of their wave front sets does not intersect the zero-section (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"}> Hörmander's criterion
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"} Hörmander 90, theorem 8.2.10
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#motivation Motivation
          %p
            The definition of wavefront sets is motivated by a version of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paley-Wiener+theorem"} Paley-Wiener theorem
            that characterizes smooth compactly supported functions (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}
            ) by a growth condition on their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paley-Wiener-Schwartz+theorem"}> Paley-Wiener-Schwartz theorem
                )
            %p
              The vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^n)
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compactly supported
              functions (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+functions"}> bump functions
              ) is (algebraically and topologically)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entire+functions"} entire functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              which satisfy the following estimate: there is a positive constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              such that for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \gt 0
              there is a constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_m
              such that:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi m
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F(z) \le C_m (1 + |z|)^{-m} \exp{ (B \; |\operatorname{Im}(z)|)}
          %h3#smoothness Smoothness
          %p
            We call a smooth compactly supported function that is identically
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            in a neighbourhood of a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            a
            %strong cutoff
            function at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \mathbb{R}^n
            be open, we identify the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times \mathbb{R}^n
            is said to be
            %strong conic
            if it is stable under the transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ζ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mi ζ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext with
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (x, \zeta) \mapsto (x, \rho \zeta) \quad \text{with} \; \rho \gt 0
          %p
            Note that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+set"} conic
            subset is uniquely determined by its intersection with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+sphere"} unit sphere
            bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\times S^{n-1}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              be a distribution and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ζ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_0, \zeta_0)
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ζ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta_0 \neq 0
              be a point of the cotangent bundle of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is
              %strong smooth
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ζ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_0, \zeta_0)
              if there is a cutoff function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
              and an open cone
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_0
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              containing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ζ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta_0
              such that for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \gt 0
              there is a nonnegative constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_m
              such that for all
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ζ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta \in \Gamma_0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi χ
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ζ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi ζ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\| \mathcal{F}(\chi f) (\zeta)   \| \le C_m (1 + \| \zeta \|)^{-m}
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi χ
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}(\chi f)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
              (of the variable
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ζ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi f
              (of the variable
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A distribution
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is smooth in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+set"} conic
              subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              of the cotangent bundle of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is smooth in a neighbourhood of every point in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %h3#wavefront_set Wavefront set
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n
            be an open subset,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* U
            its cotangent bundle and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            be a distribution on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+set"} conic
            subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* U
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is smooth is the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                wavefront set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WF(f)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+set"} conic
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* U
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander70"}> Hörmander 70 (2.4.1)
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"} Hörmander 90, section 8.1
          %p
            This definition turns out to make invariant sense (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, p. 256
            ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          #WaveFrontOfDeltaDistribution.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong
                (wave front set of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+distribution"} delta distribution
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+distribution"} delta distribution
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta(0) \in \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)
            %p
              on
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
              at the origin. Its wave front set is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo }
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(\delta(0))
                    \=
                    \\left\{
                    (0,k)
                    \\;\vert\;
                    k \in \mathbb{R}^n \setminus \{0\}
                    \\right\}
                    \\subset
                    \\mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n
                    \\simeq
                    T^\ast \mathbb{R}^n
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First of all the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support+of+a+distribution"} singular support
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(0)
              is clearly
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi singsupp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} singsupp(\delta(0)) = \{0\}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \setminus \{0\}
            %p
              At the origin, any bump function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              supported around the origin with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(0) = 1
              satisfies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \cdot \delta(0) = \delta(0)
              and hence the wave front set over the origin is the set of covectors along which the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transform
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \delta(0)
              does not suitably decay. But this Fourier transform is in fact a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
              and hence does not decay in any direction.
          #WaveFrontSetOfHeavisideDistribution.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (wave front set of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heaviside+distribution"} Heaviside distribution
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^1)
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heaviside+distribution"} Heaviside distribution
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ≔
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mn 0
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi d
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle H, b\rangle \coloneqq \int_0^\infty b(x)\, d x
                    \\,.
            %p Its wave front set is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(H) = \{(0,k) \vert k \neq 0\}
                    \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,e)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetime"} globally hyperbolic spacetime
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+differential+operator"} hyperbolic differential operator
              such as the
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+operator"} wave operator
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of singularities theorem
              says that the wave front set of any solution
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P f = 0
              is a union of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesics"} geodesics
              and their
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+vectors"} cotangent vectors
            %p
              Specifically for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
              such ditributional solutions include the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
              and the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
          #WaveFrontOfTensorProductDistribution.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (wave front set of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+distribution"} tensor product distribution
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in \mathcal{D}'(X)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in \mathcal{D}'(Y)
              be two distributions. then the wave front set of their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+distribution"} tensor product distribution
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \otimes v \in \mathcal{D}'(X \times Y)
              satisfies
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mo ∪
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi supp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi u
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ×
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mo ∪
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi supp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ×
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(u \otimes v)
                    \\;\subset\;
                    \\left(
                    WF(u) \times WF(v)
                    \\right)
                    \\cup
                    \\left(
                    \\left( supp(u) \times \{0\} \right)
                    \\times WF(v)
                    \\right)
                    \\cup
                    \\left(
                    WF(u)
                    \\times
                    \\left(
                    supp(v) \times \{0\}
                    \\right)
                    \\right)
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi supp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} supp(-)
              denotes the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support+of+a+distribution"} support of a distribution
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, theorem 8.2.9
            )
          %h2#properties Properties
          #EmptyWaveFrontSetCorrespondsToOrdinaryFunction.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (empty wave front set corresponds to ordinary functions)
            %p
              The wave front set of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+supported+distribution"} compactly supported distribution
              is empty precisely if the distribution comes from an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              (hence a
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
          %p
            e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, below (8.1.1)
            )
          #DerivativeOfDistributionRetainsOrShrinksWaveFrontSet.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative+of+distributions"} derivative of distributions
                retains or shrinks wave front set)
            %p
              Taking
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives+of+distributions"} derivatives of distributions
              retains or shrinks the wave front set:
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)
              a distribution and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in \mathbb{N}^n
              a multi-index with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^\alpha
              denoting the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivative"} partial
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative+of+distributions"} derivative of distributions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi α
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(D^\alpha u) \subset WF(u)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Hence if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(P u) \subset WF(u)
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, (8.1.10) (8.1.11), p. 256
            )
          #WaveFrontSetOfCompactlySupportedDistributions.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+of+distributions"} convolution of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+supported+distributions"} compactly supported distributions
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u,v \in \mathcal{E}'(\mathbb{R}^n)
              be two
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+supported+distributions"} compactly supported distributions
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
              of their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+of+distributions"} convolution of distributions
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mtext and
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi WF
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(u \star v)
                    \\;=\;
                    \\left\{
                    (x + y, k)
                    \\;\vert\;
                    (x,k) \in WF(u) \,\text{and}\, (y,k) \in WF(u)
                    \\right\}
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "convolution+of+distributions#Bengel77"}> Bengel 77, prop. 3.1
            )
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+divergence"} ultraviolet divergence
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The concept of wave front set is due to
          %ul
            %li#Hoermander70
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear differential operators
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathunion.org/ICM/ICM1970.1/Main/icm1970.1.0121.0134.ocr.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A textbook account for distributions on open subsets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Hoermander90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
              , section 8.1 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em The analysis of linear partial differential operators
          %p and for distributions more generally on smooth manifolds is in
          %ul
            %li#Hoermander94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The analysis of linear partial differential operators
          %p
            A history of the concept of wave front sets with extensive pointers to the literature is given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"} Hörmander 90, p. 322-324
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia:
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wavefront_set"} wavefront set
          %h3#in_quantum_field_theory In quantum field theory
          %p
            The application of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            via wave front sets to the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and especially
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory+on+curved+spacetimes"} quantum field theory on curved spacetimes
            originates with the results of
          %ul
            %li#DuistermaatHoermander72
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johann+Duistermaat"}> Johann Duistermaat
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"} Lars Hörmander
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fourier integral operators II
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.acta/1485889724"} Euclid
          %p which were first picked up in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                C. Moreno,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Spaces of positive and negative frequency solutions of field equations in curved space- times. I. The Klein-Gordon equation in stationary space-times, II. The massive vector field equations in static space-times
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Dimock"}> Jonathan Dimock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Scalar quantum field in an external gravitational background
          %p
            and brought into context with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+distributions"} Hadamard distributions
            needed for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/construction"} construction
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            in
          %ul
            %li#Radzikowski96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marek+Radzikowski"}> Marek Radzikowski
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Micro-local approach to the Hadamard condition in quantum field theory on curved space-time
                %strong 179
                (1996) 529-553 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02100096"}> doi:10.1007/BF02100096
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104287114"} euclid:cmp/1104287114
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
                %strong 208
                (2000) 623-661 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"}> math-ph/9903028
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s002200050004"} doi:10.1007/s002200050004
          %p
            A textbook account amplifying this usage (on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
            %li#Scharf01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
